1. Abstract

Water-leak detection by acoustic and vibration methods is a mature field [P]. This document does not claim that invention.

v0.4 corrects the mathematical errors of v0.3 and completes the theoretical framework. The four corrections are:

1. Source physics. The Ffowcs Williams–Hawkings monopole/dipole/quadrupole decomposition is retained, but the link to wall vibration is made dimensionally explicit via Curle's extension for solid-boundary loading. The monopole term is not dismissed; it is assigned a cavitation-state parameter. The numerical evaluation of η_vib at M = 0.0165 is corrected.
2. Detection theory. The CUSUM optimality statement is sharpened to the exact Moustakides (1986) result for i.i.d. observations and the Lai (1998) extension to non-i.i.d. pre-change baselines. The multi-stream fusion theory — which is the mathematical backbone of the architecture — is stated and proved. The window-vote heuristic is formally replaced by a fusion rule with a false-alarm guarantee.
3. Energy-constrained detection. Duty cycling is no longer an engineering assumption; it is a theorem. The energy–delay–P_D frontier is derived from the deflection coefficient and the Neyman–Pearson wake-up comparator.
4. Localization. The CRLB for TDOA is corrected for the random-signal (WSS Gaussian) model, and the velocity-uncertainty term is included. The spatial error bound is a sum of timing error and sound-speed error, and the latter dominates on plastic pipes.

The central falsifier is unchanged and is now derived rather than assumed:

\boxed{\;\eta_{\text{vib}}(M) \;\sim\; C_d\,M^{3}\;\;\text{(sharp edge)} \quad\text{or}\quad C_d\,M^{4}\;\;\text{(rounded edge)}\;}

At M=0.016, this places η at the boundary of the v0.3 kill criterion. The entire project hinges on whether cavitation or wall resonance lifts it above 10^{-6} in the band the sensor can actually see. That is a bench measurement, not a simulation.

