3. Physical Model

3.1 Pipe–fluid system

Let the plumbing network contain N nodes. The hydraulic state at time t is

u(t) = \big(q(t),\, p(t),\, T(t),\, L(t)\big)

with q flow rate, p pressure, T temperature, and L(t) \in \{0,1\} the leak indicator. Node i observes

x_i(t) = h_i\big(u(t),\, \theta_i\big) + n_i(t)

where h_i is the (nonlinear, non-stationary) transfer function from hydraulic state to sensor voltage and n_i(t) is sensor + environmental noise.

3.2 Source decomposition — Curle-extended FW-H

Proposition 3.1 (Source scaling at low Mach number). [D]

For a compact source of characteristic size d and velocity U in a fluid of density \rho_0 and sound speed c_0, the radiated acoustic power decomposes into three terms whose leading-order Mach scalings are

W_{\text{monopole}} \;=\; C_m\,\rho_0\,U^{3}d^{2}\,M,
\qquad
W_{\text{dipole}} \;=\; C_d\,\rho_0\,U^{3}d^{2}\,M^{3},
\qquad
W_{\text{quadrupole}} \;=\; C_q\,\rho_0\,U^{3}d^{2}\,M^{5},

with M = U/c_0 and C_m, C_d, C_q dimensionless coefficients of order unity that depend on geometry.

Proof. The FW-H equation

\Box^{2} p' \;=\; \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\!\left[\rho_0 v_n\,\delta(f)\right]
\;-\; \frac{\partial}{\partial x_i}\!\left[l_i\,\delta(f)\right]
\;+\; \frac{\partial^{2}}{\partial x_i \partial x_j}\!\left[T_{ij}\,H(f)\right]

with the free-space Green's function G = \delta(t - |x|/c_0)/(4\pi|x|) yields three retarded integrals. In the compact-source limit d \ll \lambda:

Monopole. The volume source Q \sim U d^{2} has time derivative \dot Q \sim U^{2}d, giving pressure

p' \sim \frac{\rho_0 \dot Q}{4\pi r} = \frac{\rho_0 U^{2}d}{4\pi r}.

Acoustic intensity I = p'^{2}/(\rho_0 c_0), and power W = 4\pi r^{2}I, so

W_{\rm mono} \sim \frac{\rho_0 U^{4}d^{2}}{c_0} = \rho_0 U^{3}d^{2}M.

Dipole. The fluctuating force on the orifice edge is F \sim \rho_0 U^{2}d^{2} (drag scale). Curle's extension adds the surface term

p' \sim \frac{1}{4\pi r c_0}\frac{\partial F}{\partial t}
\sim \frac{\omega F}{4\pi r c_0}
\sim \frac{U}{d}\frac{\rho_0 U^{2}d^{2}}{4\pi r c_0}
= \frac{\rho_0 U^{3}d}{4\pi r c_0}.

Then

W_{\rm dip} \sim \frac{p'^{2}r^{2}}{\rho_0 c_0}
\sim \frac{\rho_0 U^{6}d^{2}}{c_0^{3}}
= \rho_0 U^{3}d^{2}M^{3}.

Quadrupole. The Lighthill stress T_{ij} \sim \rho_0 U^{2} has second time derivative \partial_t^{2}T \sim \omega^{2}\rho_0 U^{2} \sim \rho_0 U^{4}/d^{2}, giving

p' \sim \frac{\rho_0 U^{4}d^{2}}{4\pi r c_0^{2}d^{2}} = \frac{\rho_0 U^{4}}{4\pi r c_0^{2}},

and

W_{\rm quad} \sim \rho_0 U^{3}d^{2}M^{5}.

At M \ll 1, the ordering is monopole \gg dipole \gg quadrupole by factors of M^{-2} each. \square

Corollary 3.2. For a water leak at line pressure p, the jet velocity at the vena contracta is

U \;=\; \sqrt{\frac{2p}{\rho_0}} \;\approx\; 24.5\ \text{m/s}\quad\text{at }p = 3\ \text{bar},

hence M \approx 0.0165 and

M \;=\; 1.65\times 10^{-2},\qquad
M^{3} \;=\; 4.49\times 10^{-6},\qquad
M^{5} \;=\; 1.22\times 10^{-9}.

Consequence. At a water-leak Mach number the quadrupole (Lighthill) channel is suppressed by nine orders of magnitude relative to the base kinetic-energy flux. The v0.2 formula W_{\text{ac}} = K\rho_0 c_0 U^{8}d^{2} is dimensionally wrong and physically inapplicable. The dominant radiating mechanism at M \ll 1 is the dipole: the fluctuating force of the jet on the orifice edge and pipe wall.

3.3 Efficiency η — three definitions

Three different efficiencies are conflated in v0.2 and v0.3. v0.4 separates them.

Symbol Definition Physical meaning
\eta_{\text{rad}} W_{\text{ac}}/(Q_\ell p) Fraction of hydraulic power radiated as far-field sound
\eta_{\text{vib}} P_{\text{wall}}/(Q_\ell p) Fraction delivered to pipe-wall mechanical vibration
\eta_{\text{sig}} P_{\text{sensor}}/(Q_\ell p) Fraction delivered to the sensor's electrical output

Only \eta_{\text{vib}} is measurable with a clamp-on accelerometer. \eta_{\text{rad}} is measurable only with a hydrophone in a free field. \eta_{\text{sig}} includes the coupling transfer function |H_{\text{clamp}}(f)|^{2}, which is a mounting property, not a leak property.

Proposition 3.3 (Dipole efficiency into wall vibration). [D]

For a sharp-edged orifice, the fluctuating force on the edge is F \sim C_d \rho_0 U^{2}d^{2}. The mechanical power delivered to the wall is

P_{\text{wall}} = \frac{\omega^{2}|F|^{2}}{12\pi\rho_0 c_0^{3}} \cdot T_{\text{wall}},

where T_{\text{wall}} is the wall transmission coefficient (dimensionless, \leq 1). The hydraulic power is

Q_\ell p = \frac{\pi}{8}\rho_0 U^{3}d^{2}.

Thus

\eta_{\text{vib}} = \frac{P_{\text{wall}}}{Q_\ell p}
= \frac{8C_d^{2}}{3\pi^{2}}M^{3}\,T_{\text{wall}}
\equiv C_{\text{vib}}\,M^{3},

with C_{\text{vib}} = 8C_d^{2}T_{\text{wall}}/(3\pi^{2}).

For a rounded-edge orifice, the loading is smoother: F \sim C_d'\rho_0 U^{2}d^{2}M (the extra factor of M from weaker separation), giving

\eta_{\text{vib}} = C_{\text{vib}}'\,M^{4}.

Proof. Standard dipole radiation formula (Curle 1955; Howe 2003) with the Strouhal frequency \omega \sim U/d. The M^{3}/M^{4} split follows from the difference between the Powell–Howe vortex-sound source at a sharp edge and a smoothly contoured edge. The transmission coefficient T_{\text{wall}} is measured, not derived. \square

Numerical consequence. At M = 0.0165:

M^{3} = 4.49\times 10^{-6},\qquad
M^{4} = 7.41\times 10^{-8}.

With C_{\text{vib}} \sim 1 (sharp edge, T_{\text{wall}} \sim 0.1–1):

\eta_{\text{vib}}^{\text{sharp}} \approx 4.5\,C_{\text{vib}} \times 10^{-7}
\;\text{to}\;
4.5\,C_{\text{vib}} \times 10^{-6}.

For a rounded edge:

\eta_{\text{vib}}^{\text{rounded}} \approx 7.4\,C_{\text{vib}}' \times 10^{-9}
\;\text{to}\;
7.4\,C_{\text{vib}}' \times 10^{-8}.

The sharp-edge case sits at or below the v0.2 kill threshold of 10^{-6} depending on T_{\text{wall}}. The rounded-edge case is one to two orders below. The project does not have a factor of safety here. Every experiment in §6 is designed to measure this number, not to confirm it.

3.4 Monopole and cavitation

Hypothesis 3.4. [H] For a leak at pressure p whose vena contracta pressure drops below the vapor pressure p_v, the collapse of cavitation bubbles radiates as a monopole with efficiency

\eta_{\text{cav}} \;\sim\; C_{\text{cav}}\,\frac{p - p_v}{p}\,\cdot\,\frac{\rho_v}{\rho_0}\,\cdot\,(\text{collapse factor}),

which can exceed \eta_{\text{vib}} by two to three orders of magnitude in the kHz band. The cavitation number is

\sigma \;=\; \frac{p - p_v}{\tfrac{1}{2}\rho_0 U^{2}}.

At 3 bar line pressure, \sigma \approx 1 — right at the cavitation onset. A leak at 3 bar may or may not cavitate depending on the vena-contracta contraction coefficient, which is a geometry-dependent quantity. A single scalar η does not exist. η must be reported as a family \eta(p, d, \text{edge}, T, \sigma).

This resolves a discrepancy in v0.2: Hunaidi's observation that leak-noise spectral content shifts with pressure [P] is explicable under dipole + cavitation theory but not under a pure U^{8} quadrupole law.

3.5 Propagation

Korteweg (fluid-borne, low-frequency limit). [P]

c \;=\; \frac{c_0}{\sqrt{1 + \dfrac{K D}{E e}}},\qquad c_0 = \sqrt{K/\rho_0}.

Empirical (leak-noise, wall-borne, band-averaged). [P] Reported attenuation is approximately:

Material Attenuation (dB/m) Useful band (Hz)
Ductile iron 0.89 500–5000
Cast iron 2.42 500–5000
PVC 5.50 50–800
PEX 5–10 50–600

Note. The accelerometer on the outer wall of the pipe sees shell modes, not the fluid breathing mode. The table is empirical and should not be used for localization on plastic pipes without dispersion correction.

Frequency dependence. [P] Attenuation in plastic pipes is strongly frequency-dependent: \alpha(f) \propto f^{1} to f^{2} depending on material and burial. This must be modelled as \alpha(f, M_{\text{mat}}), not a constant.

3.6 The full link budget — corrected

Proposition 3.5 (Detection condition). [D]

A leak of rate Q_\ell at distance L from the sensor is detectable in band B iff the acceleration power spectral density at the sensor exceeds the sensor noise floor:

S_{aa}^{\text{leak}}(f) \;>\; S_{aa}^{\text{noise}}(f)

for some non-empty band B, after integration time T such that T \cdot B \gg 1.

The leak acceleration PSD is constructed from the source pressure PSD through the transfer chain:

S_{aa}^{\text{leak}}(f)
=
|H_{\text{sensor}}(f)|^{2}
\cdot
|H_{\text{clamp}}(f)|^{2}
\cdot
|H_{\text{wall}}(f)|^{2}
\cdot
S_{pp}^{\text{source}}(f)
\cdot
e^{-2\alpha(f)L}.

The source pressure PSD is

S_{pp}^{\text{source}}(f)
=
\frac{\eta_{\text{vib}}\,p\,Q_\ell}{2\pi f\,B}
\;\;[\text{Pa}^{2}/\text{Hz}].

The units are explicit:

· S_{pp}^{\text{source}}: Pa²/Hz
· H_{\text{wall}}: (m/s²)/Pa — wall acceleration per unit source pressure
· H_{\text{clamp}}: dimensionless — mounting insertion loss
· H_{\text{sensor}}: V/(m/s²) — accelerometer sensitivity
· S_{aa}^{\text{leak}}: (m/s²)²/Hz

Equivalently, converting to acceleration spectral density,

S_{aa}^{\text{leak}}(f) \;=\;
\frac{\eta_{\text{vib}}\,p\,Q_\ell}{2\pi f\,B} \cdot
e^{-2\alpha(f)L}\cdot
|H_{\text{clamp}}(f)|^{2}\cdot
|H_{\text{wall}}(f)|^{2}
\;\;[\text{(m/s}^{2}\text{)}^{2}/\text{Hz}].

This is the single most important equation in the document. Every falsification criterion in §7 is a corollary of it. Every experimental measurement in §6 is a measurement of one of its factors.

