4. Mathematical Framework

4.1 Feature manifold

For each observation window W_k = [t_k - T_w, t_k], node i computes

z_i(k) \;=\; \big[\,E_i,\; f_{p,i},\; BW_i,\; R_i,\; K_i,\; A_i,\; T_i,\; \text{ctx}_k\,\big] \in \mathbb{R}^{d_z}

where \text{ctx}_k is a context vector (time-of-day, temperature, inferred flow regime) that conditions the baseline. Inclusion of ctx is mandatory.

The normal manifold is

\mathcal{M}_0 \;=\; \{\, \mu_0(\text{ctx}) \;:\; \text{ctx} \in \mathcal{C} \,\}

a (possibly) low-dimensional family of context-conditioned means. Local anomaly score:

D_i(k) \;=\; \sqrt{ \bigl(z_i(k) - \hat\mu_0(\text{ctx}_k)\bigr)^{\!\top}\, \hat\Sigma_0^{-1}\, \bigl(z_i(k) - \hat\mu_0(\text{ctx}_k)\bigr) }.

4.2 Change-point detection — exact and asymptotic optimality

Definition 4.1. Under H_0, z_k \sim P_0 (baseline). Under H_1, at some unknown time \nu, z_k \sim P_1 for k \geq \nu. The expected detection delay (EDD) is \mathbb{E}_\nu[\tau_d - \nu \mid \tau_d \geq \nu] and the average run length under H_0 is \text{ARL}_0 = \mathbb{E}_\infty[\tau_d].

Proposition 4.2 (Moustakides optimality, i.i.d. case). [P]

For i.i.d. observations with known P_0 and P_1, among all stopping rules with \text{ARL}_0 \geq \gamma, the CUSUM rule

S_k = \max\!\bigl(0,\; S_{k-1} + \ell_k\bigr),\qquad
\ell_k = \log\frac{dP_1}{dP_0}(z_k),\qquad
\tau_d = \inf\{k : S_k > h\}

is exactly optimal in the Lorden minimax sense for every fixed \gamma > 1, with h = \log \gamma. The optimal EDD is

\inf_{\tau} \sup_{\nu} \,\mathbb{E}_\nu[\tau_d - \nu \mid \tau_d \geq \nu]
\;=\;
\frac{\log \gamma}{D(P_1 \,\|\, P_0)}\,\bigl(1 + o(1)\bigr)
\quad\text{as }\gamma \to \infty.

Proof. Moustakides (1986) established exact optimality of CUSUM for every fixed ARL constraint \gamma > 1 under Lorden's criterion, using optimal stopping theory. The asymptotic lower bound is Lorden (1971). \square

Proposition 4.3 (Lai extension to non-i.i.d. pre-change). [P]

For non-i.i.d. observations with dependent pre-change structure, if the average log-likelihood ratio between P_1 and P_0 converges to a constant in probability, the CUSUM procedure remains first-order asymptotically minimax optimal:

\inf_{\tau: \text{ARL}_0 \geq \gamma} \text{WADD}(\tau)
\;\sim\;
\frac{|\log \gamma|}{D}
\quad\text{as }\gamma \to \infty,

where D is the limiting average KL divergence.

Proof. Lai (1998), building on Lorden (1971). \square

Corollary 4.4. Any thresholding-on-window-vote detector of the form used in v0.2 §4.3 is dominated in the limit by CUSUM at the same ARL₀. v0.2's detector is not asymptotically optimal. v0.4 replaces it.

4.3 Multi-stream fusion — the mathematical backbone

Proposition 4.5 (Multi-stream detection). [D]

Let M nodes each run a local CUSUM with statistics S_k^{(i)}, i = 1,\ldots,M. Define three fusion rules:

· \tau_{\min} = \min_i \tau_i (first alarm)
· \tau_{\max} = \max_i \tau_i (all alarms)
· \tau_{\text{sum}} = \inf\{k : \sum_{i=1}^{M} S_k^{(i)} > h_{\text{sum}}\} (sum-of-CUSUMs)

For fixed M and as \gamma \to \infty, \tau_{\min} is second-order asymptotically optimal under Lorden's criterion when the leak signal is present at all nodes. \tau_{\text{sum}} is optimal when the leak signal is present at only a subset of nodes with unknown subset identity.

Proof sketch. The lower bound follows from the multi-stream extension of Lai (1998): the information available at the fusion center is the sum of per-stream KL divergences. \tau_{\min} attains the bound when the alternative is "all streams changed." \tau_{\text{sum}} attains the bound when the alternative is "at least one stream changed" with unknown identity; the sum-LLR accumulates evidence from all streams and is optimal in the Mei (2010) sense for sparse alternatives. \square

Consequence. The persistence-vote rule \sum A_i(k) \geq \alpha L in v0.3 §4.3 is a heuristic with no false-alarm guarantee. v0.4 replaces it with either \tau_{\text{all}} (provably optimal fusion, directly answers RQ5) or \tau_{\text{sum}}, with the persistence window retained only as a post-alarm confirmation stage.

4.4 Duty-cycled sampling — proof

Proposition 4.6. [D]

Suppose the node observes with probability p_{\text{duty}} per unit wall-clock time, so that in a window of N sample periods it makes N p_{\text{duty}} observations. Then with CUSUM applied to the observed subsequence,

\text{EDD}_{\text{wall}} \;\sim\; \frac{h}{p_{\text{duty}}\,D(P_1 \,\|\, P_0)} \cdot T_s,
\qquad
\text{ARL}_{0,\text{wall}} \;\sim\; \frac{e^{h}}{p_{\text{duty}}} \cdot T_s,

where T_s is the sample period. Consequently the ratio EDD / log(ARL₀) is invariant under duty cycling:

\frac{\text{EDD}_{\text{wall}}}{\log \text{ARL}_{0,\text{wall}}} \;\sim\; \frac{1}{D(P_1 \,\|\, P_0)}.

Proof. Let \{z_k\} be the full observation sequence and \{z_{k_j}\} the subsequence actually observed, with \mathbb{E}[\text{card}\{k_j \leq n\}] = p_{\text{duty}} n. The CUSUM statistic on the subsequence is

S_{k_j} = \max\!\left(0, S_{k_{j-1}} + \log\frac{dP_1}{dP_0}(z_{k_j})\right).

Under H_1, \mathbb{E}_1[S_{k_j}] = j \cdot D(P_1\|P_0); under H_0, \mathbb{E}_0[S_{k_j}] = -j \cdot D(P_0\|P_1). The Wald approximation gives ARL₀ = e^{h} and EDD = h/D(P_1\|P_0) in units of observations. Converting to wall-clock time: each observation takes T_s/p_{\text{duty}} on average, so EDD_{\text{wall}} = h T_s/(p_{\text{duty}}D) and ARL_{0,\text{wall}} = e^{h}T_s/p_{\text{duty}}. The ratio EDD/log(ARL₀) is 1/D, independent of p_{\text{duty}}. \square

Consequence. Duty cycling does not degrade the fundamental detection trade-off; it only rescales absolute timescales. This justifies the 1 % duty cycle as lossless from a detection-theory standpoint.

4.5 Manifold-valued statistics

Proposition 4.7 (Data-processing bound). [D]

Let D_k = d(\phi(z_k), \mathcal{M}_0) be the manifold-distance statistic with \phi a fixed feature extractor. If \phi is sufficient for \{P_0, P_1\} in the Fisher–Neyman sense, then D(P_1^{D} \,\|\, P_0^{D}) = D(P_1^{z} \,\|\, P_0^{z}). Otherwise,

D\!\left(P_1^{D} \,\big\|\, P_0^{D}\right) \;\leq\; D\!\left(P_1^{z} \,\big\|\, P_0^{z}\right),

and the CUSUM bound using D_k is weaker by the ratio of the two KL divergences.

Proof. Immediate from the data-processing inequality for f-divergences. \square

Corollary 4.8. The choice of \phi matters quantitatively, not just qualitatively. A poorly chosen \phi can shrink the effective KL divergence by orders of magnitude. RQ1 must be rephrased as: Which \phi maximizes D(P_1^{\phi} \| P_0^{\phi}) subject to on-node compute constraints?

4.6 Identifiability — physical detection threshold

Proposition 4.9 (Deflection coefficient and Q_min). [D]

For a Gaussian leak signal with PSD S_s(f) observed through transfer function H(f) in noise N(f) over time T, the energy detector's deflection coefficient is

d^2 = 2T\int_{B} \frac{|S_s(f)\,|H(f)|^2|^2}{N(f)} df,

and the detection performance is

P_D \approx Q\!\left(Q^{-1}(P_{FA}) - d\right).

From the link budget of Proposition 3.5, the minimum detectable leak rate at confidence (P_D, P_{FA}) and integration time T is

Q_\ell^{\min}(p, L, M_{\text{mat}}, T, P_D, P_{FA})
=
\frac{ z(P_D, P_{FA}) \cdot S_{aa}^{\text{noise}} \cdot B }
     { \eta_{\text{vib}}\,p\, e^{-2\alpha L}\, |H_{\text{clamp}}|^{2}\,|H_{\text{wall}}|^{2} \cdot \sqrt{T\,B} },

where z(P_D, P_{FA}) = Q^{-1}(P_{FA}) - Q^{-1}(P_D) is the standard normal quantile product.

Crucially, Q_\ell^{\min} is a random variable with distribution induced by the uncertainty in |H_{\text{clamp}}|^{2} (mounting), \eta_{\text{vib}} (cavitation state), and \alpha (material/soil). Its median and 95th percentile are both engineering-relevant. A design that meets the target at the median but fails at the 95th percentile is not a field-deployable design.

Theorem 4.10 (No-detector-beats bound). For given (p, d, M_{\text{mat}}, N) and observation budget T, no detector — with any amount of AI — can achieve a lower Q_\ell^{\min} than Proposition 4.9 at fixed (P_D, P_{FA}). CUSUM attains this bound asymptotically.

Proof. The deflection coefficient is the maximal signal-to-noise ratio achievable by any linear detector. The Neyman–Pearson lemma gives the optimal test at fixed P_{FA}. The CUSUM optimality (Proposition 4.2) gives the sequential bound. \square

4.7 Localization CRLB with coherence bandwidth — corrected

Proposition 4.11 (TDOA CRLB, random-signal model). [D]

For observations x_1(t) = s(t) + n_1(t), x_2(t) = s(t-\Delta t) + n_2(t) with s a zero-mean stationary Gaussian process of PSD S_s(f) and independent noise PSDs S_{n_1}(f), S_{n_2}(f), the Fisher information on \Delta t is

\mathcal{I}(\Delta t) = 2\pi^{2}\int f^{2}\,\frac{S_s^{2}(f)}{S_{n_1}(f)S_{n_2}(f) + S_s(f)(S_{n_1}(f)+S_{n_2}(f))}\,df

(Kay 1993, §7.4, generalized). Writing \text{SNR}(f) = S_s(f)/S_n(f) and \gamma^{2}(f) = \text{SNR}^{2}(f)/(1+\text{SNR}(f))^{2}, one obtains

\mathcal{I}(\Delta t) = 2\pi^{2}\,T\int f^{2}\gamma^{2}(f)\text{SNR}(f)df,

and CRLB = \mathcal{I}^{-1}. For a rectangular coherence profile of width B_{\text{coh}} and effective SNR, this reduces to

\sigma_{\Delta t}^{2}
\;\geq\;
\frac{1}{2\pi\,B_{\text{coh}}\,\sqrt{\text{SNR}_{\text{eff}}\,T\,B_{\text{coh}}}},

where the coherence bandwidth is

B_{\text{coh}} \;=\; \frac{\left(\int \gamma^{2}(f)\,df\right)^{2}}{\int \gamma^{4}(f)\,df}.

The correct bandwidth is the coherence bandwidth, which is bounded above by both the source bandwidth and the dispersion-limited coherence bandwidth of the propagation channel:

B_{\text{coh}} \;\leq\; \min\!\left(B_{\text{source}},\; B_{\text{dispersion}},\; B_{\text{multipath}}\right).

Proof. Standard CRLB for time-delay estimation applied to a dispersive channel; the coherence weighting arises from the fact that only coherent energy contributes to the delay information. The random-signal model is required because leak noise is WSS Gaussian, not deterministic. \square

Proposition 4.12 (Spatial error including velocity uncertainty). [D]

The total spatial error is

\sigma_{x_\ell}^2 = \frac{c^2}{4}\sigma_{\Delta t}^2 + \frac{\Delta t^2}{4}\sigma_c^2.

On plastic pipes, \sigma_c/c \sim 5–10\% easily dominates. A 4 ms TDOA with 10% velocity error gives ±13 cm before any timing error.

Numerical consequence.

Material B_{\text{source}} B_{\text{coh}} (realistic) \sigma_{\Delta t} at SNR=0 dB, T=1s \sigma_x = c_g \sigma_{\Delta t}/2
Copper 5 kHz 500 Hz \approx 90\ \mus \approx 5.8 cm
Steel 5 kHz 500 Hz \approx 90\ \mus \approx 6.3 cm
PEX 750 Hz 30 Hz \approx 1\ \text{ms} \approx 20 cm
PVC 750 Hz 30 Hz \approx 1\ \text{ms} \approx 30 cm

The v0.2 target of "\pm 0.5 m" is achievable on all materials at T=1 s. The v0.2 implication that PEX is worse for localization is wrong: PEX has lower c_g, so for a given \sigma_{\Delta t} the spatial error is smaller. The trade-off is the opposite of what v0.2 assumed.

4.8 Cross-node consistency — corrected degrees of freedom

Definition 4.13 (Consistency test). [S]

For a set of N nodes, only N-1 TDOAs are independent. Select a reference node and compute N-1 TDOAs. A candidate leak hypothesis (x_\ell, \nu) is consistent if

\chi^{2} \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^{N-1} \bigl(\hat\tau_{i} - \tau_{i}(x_\ell)\bigr)^{\!\top}\,
\hat\Sigma_{\tau,i}^{-1}\,
\bigl(\hat\tau_{i} - \tau_{i}(x_\ell)\bigr)
\;\leq\; \chi^{2}_{1-\alpha}(N-2).

Multi-node detection requires both a local CUSUM alarm and a passing consistency test.

4.9 Energy-constrained detection — the frontier

Proposition 4.14 (Energy–delay–P_D frontier). [D]

Let the analog wake-up comparator be a Neyman–Pearson energy detector with false-wake rate \alpha_w per unit time and detection probability P_{D,w} at the target leak energy. The average power is

\bar P = D\,P_{\text{acq}} + (1-D)P_{\text{sleep}} + P_{\text{cmp}},

with D = T_{\text{acq}}/(T_{\text{acq}} + T_{\text{sleep}}). The effective integration time is T_{\text{eff}} = \rho T where \rho = p_{\text{duty}}. From Proposition 4.9, d^2 \propto \rho T, so

P_{FA} \le \alpha,\quad P_D \ge 1-\beta
\;\Longrightarrow\;
E_{\min} \propto \frac{(Q^{-1}(\alpha)-Q^{-1}(1-\beta))^2}{\rho \cdot \mathrm{SNR}^2 B}.

Proof. The deflection coefficient scales linearly with effective integration time. The energy is the product of average power and time. Inverting the detection condition gives the minimum energy. \square

Consequence. Duty cycling does not degrade the fundamental detection trade-off; it only rescales absolute timescales. This justifies the 1 % duty cycle as lossless from a detection-theory standpoint and provides a principled answer to "how little hardware is sufficient."

