Appendix B — Dipole Efficiency into Wall Vibration

B.1 Free-field dipole radiation

The acoustic power radiated by a compact dipole of force amplitude F at frequency \omega is

W_{\text{dip}} = \frac{\omega^{2}|F|^{2}}{12\pi\rho_0 c_0^{3}}.

This is the standard result from Curle (1955) and Howe (2003). The factor 12\pi arises from integrating the dipole directivity \cos^{2}\theta over the sphere.

Derivation: The far-field pressure of a dipole is

p'(r, \theta, t) = \frac{i\omega F}{4\pi r c_0}\cos\theta\,e^{i\omega(t - r/c_0)}.

The time-averaged intensity is

I(r, \theta) = \frac{|p'|^{2}}{2\rho_0 c_0} = \frac{\omega^{2}|F|^{2}}{32\pi^{2}r^{2}c_0^{2}}\frac{\cos^{2}\theta}{2\rho_0 c_0}.

Integrating over the sphere:

W = \int_{0}^{2\pi}\int_{0}^{\pi} I\,r^{2}\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\phi
= \frac{\omega^{2}|F|^{2}}{32\pi^{2}c_0^{3}\rho_0} \cdot 2\pi \cdot \int_{0}^{\pi}\cos^{2}\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta.

The angular integral is \int_{0}^{\pi}\cos^{2}\theta\sin\theta\,d\theta = 2/3. So

W = \frac{\omega^{2}|F|^{2}}{32\pi^{2}c_0^{3}\rho_0} \cdot 2\pi \cdot \frac{2}{3}
= \frac{\omega^{2}|F|^{2}}{24\pi c_0^{3}\rho_0}.

Wait — this gives 24\pi, not 12\pi. The discrepancy is a factor of 2 from whether F is the peak or RMS force, and whether the time average includes the factor of 1/2. Let me redo carefully.

Convention: Let F(t) = F_0\cos\omega t. The far-field pressure is

p'(r,\theta,t) = \frac{\cos\theta}{4\pi r c_0}\frac{dF}{dt}(t - r/c_0)
= -\frac{\omega F_0\cos\theta}{4\pi r c_0}\sin\omega(t - r/c_0).

The RMS pressure is |p'|_{\text{rms}} = \omega F_0|\cos\theta|/(4\pi r c_0\sqrt{2}).

Time-averaged intensity:

I = \frac{|p'|_{\text{rms}}^{2}}{\rho_0 c_0}
= \frac{\omega^{2}F_0^{2}\cos^{2}\theta}{32\pi^{2}r^{2}c_0^{2}\rho_0 c_0}.

Total power:

W = \int I\,r^{2}\,d\Omega
= \frac{\omega^{2}F_0^{2}}{32\pi^{2}c_0^{3}\rho_0} \cdot 2\pi \cdot \frac{2}{3}
= \frac{\omega^{2}F_0^{2}}{24\pi c_0^{3}\rho_0}.

If we define F as the RMS force F_{\text{rms}} = F_0/\sqrt{2}, then F_0^{2} = 2F_{\text{rms}}^{2}, and

W = \frac{\omega^{2}(2F_{\text{rms}}^{2})}{24\pi c_0^{3}\rho_0}
= \frac{\omega^{2}F_{\text{rms}}^{2}}{12\pi c_0^{3}\rho_0}.

So the factor of 12\pi holds when F is RMS. We adopt the RMS convention throughout.

B.2 Force scale and frequency

The fluctuating force on the orifice edge is

F_{\text{rms}} = C_d \rho_0 U^{2} d^{2}.

The Strouhal frequency is

\omega_s = 2\pi\,\text{St}\,\frac{U}{d},

with \text{St} \approx 0.2 for a circular orifice. We absorb St into C_d and write \omega \sim U/d.

B.3 Power delivered to the wall

The free-field radiation formula assumes the dipole radiates into an infinite fluid. In reality, the dipole is mounted on a pipe wall, and only a fraction T_{\text{wall}} of the radiated power enters the wall as mechanical vibration. We define

P_{\text{wall}} = T_{\text{wall}} \cdot W_{\text{dip}}.

The transmission coefficient T_{\text{wall}} depends on the impedance mismatch between water and the pipe wall, the wall thickness, and the frequency. For a steel pipe with h/a \sim 0.05 (thickness/radius), T_{\text{wall}} \sim 0.1–1 in the 1–5 kHz band. For plastic pipes, T_{\text{wall}} is smaller due to the lower acoustic impedance of the wall material.

B.4 Dipole efficiency

Substituting F_{\text{rms}} = C_d\rho_0 U^{2}d^{2} and \omega = U/d:

W_{\text{dip}} = \frac{(U/d)^{2}(C_d\rho_0 U^{2}d^{2})^{2}}{12\pi\rho_0 c_0^{3}}
= \frac{C_d^{2}\rho_0 U^{6} d^{2}}{12\pi c_0^{3}}.

The hydraulic power is P_{\text{hyd}} = \frac{\pi}{8}\rho_0 U^{3}d^{2}. So

\eta_{\text{vib}} = \frac{P_{\text{wall}}}{P_{\text{hyd}}}
= T_{\text{wall}}\frac{C_d^{2}\rho_0 U^{6}d^{2}/(12\pi c_0^{3})}{\frac{\pi}{8}\rho_0 U^{3}d^{2}}
= T_{\text{wall}}\frac{8C_d^{2}}{12\pi^{2}}\frac{U^{3}}{c_0^{3}}.

\boxed{\eta_{\text{vib}} = \frac{2C_d^{2}T_{\text{wall}}}{3\pi^{2}}M^{3} \equiv C_{\text{vib}}M^{3}}.

B.5 Rounded-edge correction

For a rounded-edge orifice (edge radius r_e \gtrsim d/2), the flow separation is delayed and the fluctuating force is weaker. The force scale becomes

F_{\text{rms}} \sim C_d' \rho_0 U^{2} d^{2} \cdot M,

where the extra factor of M arises because the unsteady loading is a higher-order effect when the edge is smooth. This gives

\eta_{\text{vib}}^{\text{rounded}} = C_{\text{vib}}' M^{4}.

Justification: For a sharp edge, the vortex shedding is locked to the edge and the force fluctuation is O(1) in M. For a rounded edge, the shedding is weaker and the force fluctuation scales as M relative to the steady drag. This is consistent with the Powell–Howe theory of vortex sound, where the source strength depends on the vorticity distribution near the edge.

B.6 Numerical evaluation

At M = 0.0165:

M^{3} = 4.49\times 10^{-6},\qquad M^{4} = 7.41\times 10^{-8}.

With C_{\text{vib}} = 2C_d^{2}T_{\text{wall}}/(3\pi^{2}):

For C_d = 1, T_{\text{wall}} = 0.5: C_{\text{vib}} = 2 \cdot 1 \cdot 0.5/(3\pi^{2}) = 0.034. Then

\eta_{\text{vib}}^{\text{sharp}} = 0.034 \times 4.49\times 10^{-6} = 1.5\times 10^{-7}.

For T_{\text{wall}} = 1: \eta_{\text{vib}}^{\text{sharp}} = 3.0\times 10^{-7}.

For the rounded edge with C_{\text{vib}}' = 0.034: \eta_{\text{vib}}^{\text{rounded}} = 2.5\times 10^{-9}.

These values are below the v0.2 kill threshold of 10^{-6}. The project hinges on whether cavitation lifts the effective \eta above this threshold.

