Appendix C — CUSUM Optimality

C.1 Problem setup

Let \{z_k\}_{k\geq 1} be a sequence of observations. Under H_0, z_k \sim P_0 i.i.d. Under H_1, there exists an unknown change time \nu such that z_k \sim P_0 for k < \nu and z_k \sim P_1 for k \geq \nu. A stopping rule \tau is a stopping time adapted to the filtration \{\mathcal{F}_k\}.

Lorden's minimax criterion:

\mathcal{R}(\tau) = \sup_{\nu \geq 1} \operatorname{ess\,sup} \mathbb{E}_\nu[(\tau - \nu + 1)^{+} \mid \mathcal{F}_{\nu-1}].

The worst-case expected detection delay, uniformly over the pre-change history.

Constraint: \text{ARL}_0 = \mathbb{E}_\infty[\tau] \geq \gamma.

C.2 CUSUM rule

Define the log-likelihood ratio increment

\ell_k = \log\frac{dP_1}{dP_0}(z_k).

The CUSUM statistic is

S_k = \max(0, S_{k-1} + \ell_k), \qquad S_0 = 0,

and the stopping time is

\tau_{\text{CUSUM}} = \inf\{k : S_k > h\}.

C.3 Moustakides' exact optimality theorem

Theorem (Moustakides 1986). For i.i.d. observations with known P_0 and P_1, and for every \gamma > 1, the CUSUM rule with threshold h chosen so that \text{ARL}_0 = \gamma minimizes Lorden's worst-case detection delay \mathcal{R}(\tau) among all stopping rules with \text{ARL}_0 \geq \gamma.

Proof sketch. The proof proceeds in three steps.

Step 1: Reduction to a Bayesian problem. Consider the Bayesian formulation where the change time \nu has a geometric prior with parameter p, and the loss is the detection delay. The optimal Bayesian rule is a likelihood-ratio test. As p \to 0, the Bayesian problem converges to the minimax problem.

Step 2: The CUSUM is the limit of Bayesian rules. For a geometric prior on \nu with parameter p, the posterior probability that a change has occurred at or before time k is proportional to

\sum_{j=1}^{k} p(1-p)^{j-1}\prod_{i=j}^{k}\frac{dP_1}{dP_0}(z_i).

The optimal Bayesian stopping time is the first k where this posterior exceeds a threshold. As p \to 0, the dominant term in the sum is the one that maximizes the product, which is exactly the CUSUM statistic S_k. So the CUSUM is the limit of Bayesian rules.

Step 3: Optimal stopping. By the general theory of optimal stopping (Shiryaev 1978), the limit of Bayesian-optimal rules is minimax-optimal. The CUSUM attains the lower bound.

A complete proof is in Moustakides (1986), Ann. Statist. 14(4), 1379–1387.

C.4 Asymptotic lower bound (Lorden 1971)

Theorem (Lorden 1971). For any stopping rule \tau with \text{ARL}_0 \geq \gamma,

\mathcal{R}(\tau) \geq \frac{\log \gamma}{D(P_1 \| P_0)}(1 + o(1)) \quad \text{as } \gamma \to \infty.

Proof. The key idea is to consider a sequence of change times \nu = 1, 2, \ldots and use the fact that the Kullback–Leibler divergence D(P_1\|P_0) bounds the rate at which the likelihood ratio can grow. The detailed proof uses Wald's identity and the optional stopping theorem. See Lorden (1971), Ann. Math. Statist. 42(6), 1897–1908.

C.5 Lai's extension to non-i.i.d. pre-change

Theorem (Lai 1998). Suppose the pre-change observations are not i.i.d. but satisfy a mixing condition, and the post-change observations are i.i.d. with distribution P_1. If the average log-likelihood ratio converges:

\frac{1}{n}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\log\frac{dP_1}{dP_0}(z_k) \xrightarrow{P} D > 0

under H_1, then the CUSUM procedure remains first-order asymptotically minimax optimal:

\inf_{\tau: \text{ARL}_0 \geq \gamma} \text{WADD}(\tau) \sim \frac{\log \gamma}{D} \quad \text{as } \gamma \to \infty.

Proof sketch. The proof uses the fact that the CUSUM statistic is a random walk with drift D under H_1 and drift -D(P_0\|P_1) under H_0. The Wald approximation gives \text{ARL}_0 \approx e^{h} and \text{EDD} \approx h/D. The lower bound follows from the same information-theoretic argument as Lorden, applied to the mixing pre-change process. See Lai (1998), IEEE Trans. Inf. Theory 44(7), 2917–2929.

C.6 Gaussian mean-shift example

For P_0 = \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^{2}) and P_1 = \mathcal{N}(\delta, \sigma^{2}):

D(P_1\|P_0) = \frac{\delta^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}.

The EDD bound is

\text{EDD} \geq \frac{2\sigma^{2}}{\delta^{2}}\log\gamma.

This is the form used in the main document.

