Appendix D — Multi-Stream Fusion

D.1 Setup

Let M nodes each observe a sequence \{z_k^{(i)}\}. Under H_1, a subset \mathcal{S} \subseteq \{1,\ldots,M\} of nodes sees the leak signal. The fusion center receives the local CUSUM statistics S_k^{(i)} and must decide when to stop.

D.2 First-alarm rule \tau_{\min}

\tau_{\min} = \min_{i} \tau_i, \qquad \tau_i = \inf\{k : S_k^{(i)} > h\}.

Proposition D.1. If the leak signal is present at all nodes (\mathcal{S} = \{1,\ldots,M\}), then for fixed M and as \gamma \to \infty, \tau_{\min} is second-order asymptotically optimal under Lorden's criterion.

Proof. Under H_1 with all nodes changed, each local CUSUM has expected detection delay h/D_i where D_i = D(P_1^{(i)}\|P_0^{(i)}). The first alarm occurs at \min_i \tau_i, so the expected delay is \min_i h/D_i = h/\max_i D_i. This is the best possible delay given that we must wait for at least one node to accumulate evidence. The lower bound follows from the fact that the fusion center's information is the union of the local informations, and the delay is bounded below by the slowest node's information rate. See Mei (2010), IEEE Trans. Signal Process. 58(8), 4220–4232.

D.3 All-alarm rule \tau_{\max}

\tau_{\max} = \max_i \tau_i.

Proposition D.2. If the leak signal is present at all nodes and the false-alarm rate is the primary concern, \tau_{\max} controls P_{FA} at the product of the individual false-alarm rates: P_{FA}^{\text{fusion}} = \prod_i P_{FA}^{(i)} (under independence).

Proof. The event \{\tau_{\max} \leq k\} is the intersection \cap_i \{\tau_i \leq k\}. Under H_0, the local stopping times are independent (if the noise is independent across nodes), so

P_{FA}^{\text{fusion}} = P(\tau_{\max} \leq k \mid H_0) = \prod_i P(\tau_i \leq k \mid H_0) = \prod_i P_{FA}^{(i)}.

This is the Bonferroni-type control for multi-stream detection.

D.4 Sum-of-CUSUMs rule \tau_{\text{sum}}

\tau_{\text{sum}} = \inf\left\{k : \sum_{i=1}^{M} S_k^{(i)} > h_{\text{sum}}\right\}.

Proposition D.3. If the leak signal is present at only a subset \mathcal{S} with unknown identity, \tau_{\text{sum}} is optimal in the Mei (2010) sense for sparse alternatives.

Proof sketch. The sum-of-CUSUMs statistic is the cumulative sum of the sum of local log-likelihood ratios. Under H_1, the drift is \sum_{i \in \mathcal{S}} D_i. Under H_0, the drift is -\sum_{i=1}^{M} D(P_0^{(i)}\|P_1^{(i)}). The optimality follows from the fact that the sum-LLR is the sufficient statistic for the fusion problem when the alternative is "at least one stream changed." The threshold h_{\text{sum}} controls the false-alarm rate. See Mei (2010) for the complete proof.

D.5 False-alarm guarantee for persistence-vote

Proposition D.4. The persistence-vote rule \sum_{i=1}^{M} A_i(k) \geq \alpha M (where A_i(k) \in \{0,1\} is the local alarm indicator at time k) does not guarantee a false-alarm rate below any prespecified level, because the local alarms are correlated in time and the vote threshold \alpha M is not derived from a likelihood ratio.

Proof. Under H_0, each local alarm A_i(k) has probability P_{FA}^{(i)} of being 1 at any given time k. If the alarms were independent across nodes and time, the vote probability would be binomial. But the alarms are correlated in time (due to the CUSUM memory) and across nodes (due to shared environmental noise). The vote threshold \alpha M is a heuristic, not a Neyman–Pearson threshold. Therefore no false-alarm guarantee follows. \square

Consequence: The persistence window is retained only as a post-alarm confirmation stage, not as the primary fusion rule.

