Appendix E — Duty-Cycled Sampling

E.1 Random thinning model

Let \{z_k\}_{k\geq 1} be the full observation sequence. The node observes with probability p_{\text{duty}} at each time step, independently. The observed subsequence is \{z_{k_j}\}_{j\geq 1}, where k_j is the j-th observation time. The expected number of observations in n time steps is

\mathbb{E}[\text{card}\{k_j \leq n\}] = n p_{\text{duty}}.

E.2 CUSUM on the subsequence

The CUSUM statistic on the observed subsequence is

S_{k_j} = \max\left(0, S_{k_{j-1}} + \log\frac{dP_1}{dP_0}(z_{k_j})\right).

Under H_1, the expected increment is D(P_1\|P_0). Under H_0, the expected increment is -D(P_0\|P_1). By Wald's identity, the expected number of observations to reach threshold h is

\mathbb{E}_1[J] = \frac{h}{D(P_1\|P_0)}, \qquad \mathbb{E}_0[J] = \frac{e^{h}}{D(P_0\|P_1)}.

E.3 Wall-clock conversion

Each observation takes T_s/p_{\text{duty}} wall-clock time on average. So

\text{EDD}_{\text{wall}} = \mathbb{E}_1[J] \cdot \frac{T_s}{p_{\text{duty}}} = \frac{h T_s}{p_{\text{duty}} D(P_1\|P_0)},

\text{ARL}_{0,\text{wall}} = \mathbb{E}_0[J] \cdot \frac{T_s}{p_{\text{duty}}} = \frac{e^{h} T_s}{p_{\text{duty}} D(P_0\|P_1)}.

E.4 Invariance of the trade-off ratio

\frac{\text{EDD}_{\text{wall}}}{\log \text{ARL}_{0,\text{wall}}}
= \frac{h T_s/(p_{\text{duty}} D(P_1\|P_0))}{\log(e^{h} T_s/(p_{\text{duty}} D(P_0\|P_1)))}
= \frac{h T_s/(p_{\text{duty}} D(P_1\|P_0))}{h + \log(T_s/(p_{\text{duty}} D(P_0\|P_1)))}.

For large h (small false-alarm rate), the \log term is negligible, and

\frac{\text{EDD}_{\text{wall}}}{\log \text{ARL}_{0,\text{wall}}} \approx \frac{T_s}{p_{\text{duty}} D(P_1\|P_0)}.

So duty cycling rescales absolute timescales but preserves the fundamental trade-off ratio.

E.5 Caveats

1. Deterministic duty cycling: If the duty cycle is phase-locked to periodic noise (e.g., HVAC cycling), aliasing can occur. Use randomized duty cycling or ensure the duty period is incommensurate with the noise period.
2. Intermittent leaks: The persistent-change assumption may fail for intermittent leaks. The CUSUM should be augmented with a "restart" mechanism or a two-sided test.
3. Baseline learning: Duty cycling also affects the baseline learning phase. The baseline must be learned during periods when no leak is present, which requires a longer calibration period.

