Appendix F — Manifold Statistics and Data-Processing

F.1 Feature extractor and manifold distance

Let \phi: \mathbb{R}^{d_z} \to \mathbb{R}^{d_\phi} be a feature extractor. The manifold distance is

D_k = d(\phi(z_k), \mathcal{M}_0),

where \mathcal{M}_0 = \{\mu_0(\text{ctx}) : \text{ctx} \in \mathcal{C}\} is the context-conditioned normal manifold.

F.2 Data-processing inequality

Theorem F.1. For any f-divergence D_f, and any measurable function \phi,

D_f(P_1^{\phi} \| P_0^{\phi}) \leq D_f(P_1^{z} \| P_0^{z}).

Proof. The f-divergence is defined as

D_f(P\|Q) = \int f\left(\frac{dP}{dQ}\right)dQ.

For the pushforward measures P^{\phi} = P \circ \phi^{-1}, the Radon–Nikodym derivative satisfies

\frac{dP^{\phi}}{dQ^{\phi}}(\phi(z)) = \mathbb{E}\left[\frac{dP}{dQ}(z) \mid \phi(z)\right]

(by the conditional expectation property of Radon–Nikodym derivatives). By Jensen's inequality for convex f,

f\left(\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{dP}{dQ} \mid \phi\right]\right) \leq \mathbb{E}\left[f\left(\frac{dP}{dQ}\right) \mid \phi\right].

Integrating over Q^{\phi}:

D_f(P^{\phi}\|Q^{\phi}) = \int f\left(\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{dP}{dQ} \mid \phi\right]\right)dQ^{\phi}
\leq \int \mathbb{E}\left[f\left(\frac{dP}{dQ}\right) \mid \phi\right]dQ^{\phi}
= D_f(P\|Q).

\square

F.3 Sufficiency and equality

Definition F.2. \phi is sufficient for \{P_0, P_1\} in the Fisher–Neyman sense if the likelihood ratio dP_1/dP_0 is \sigma(\phi)-measurable.

Proposition F.3. If \phi is sufficient, then D_f(P_1^{\phi}\|P_0^{\phi}) = D_f(P_1^{z}\|P_0^{z}) for all f-divergences.

Proof. If \phi is sufficient, then dP_1/dP_0 = g(\phi(z)) for some function g. The pushforward measures have Radon–Nikodym derivative dP_1^{\phi}/dP_0^{\phi} = g. The f-divergence is preserved. \square

F.4 Consequence for feature design

The choice of \phi matters quantitatively. A poorly chosen \phi can shrink the effective KL divergence by orders of magnitude. The optimal \phi maximizes D(P_1^{\phi}\|P_0^{\phi}) subject to on-node compute constraints. This is RQ1.

