Appendix G — Identifiability and Deflection Coefficient

G.1 Detection problem

Consider the hypothesis test:

H_0: x[n] = w[n], \qquad H_1: x[n] = s[n] + w[n],

where s[n] is the leak signal with PSD S_s(f) and w[n] is noise with PSD S_n(f). The observation time is T samples.

G.2 Deflection coefficient

For a linear detector with test statistic T(x) = \sum_n h[n]x[n], the deflection coefficient is

d^2 = \frac{(\mathbb{E}_1[T] - \mathbb{E}_0[T])^{2}}{\text{Var}_0[T]}.

For the matched filter h[n] = s[n] (in white noise) or the whitened matched filter (in colored noise), the deflection coefficient is

d^2 = \sum_{n} \frac{|s[n]|^{2}}{\sigma_w^{2}} = \frac{T}{2}\int_{-B/2}^{B/2}\frac{S_s(f)}{S_n(f)}df.

For the general colored-noise case with transfer function H(f):

d^2 = 2T\int_{B}\frac{|S_s(f)|H(f)|^{2}|^{2}}{S_n(f)}df.

G.3 Detection performance

For a Gaussian test statistic, the detection probability is

P_D = Q\left(Q^{-1}(P_{FA}) - d\right),

where Q(\cdot) is the standard normal tail probability.

G.4 Inversion for Q_\ell^{\min}

From the link budget, the leak acceleration PSD is proportional to Q_\ell:

S_{aa}^{\text{leak}}(f) = Q_\ell \cdot \frac{\eta_{\text{vib}}\,p}{2\pi f B} \cdot e^{-2\alpha(f)L} \cdot |H_{\text{clamp}}(f)|^{2} \cdot |H_{\text{wall}}(f)|^{2}.

The deflection coefficient is proportional to Q_\ell:

d^2 = Q_\ell^{2} \cdot 2T\int_{B}\frac{|\Psi(f)|^{2}}{S_{aa}^{\text{noise}}(f)}df,

where \Psi(f) is the transfer function from Q_\ell to acceleration.

Setting d = Q^{-1}(P_{FA}) - Q^{-1}(P_D):

Q_\ell^{\min} = \frac{Q^{-1}(P_{FA}) - Q^{-1}(P_D)}{\sqrt{2T\int_{B}|\Psi(f)|^{2}/S_{aa}^{\text{noise}}(f)df}}.

In the simplified case where \Psi(f) and S_{aa}^{\text{noise}}(f) are flat over the band B:

Q_\ell^{\min} = \frac{(Q^{-1}(P_{FA}) - Q^{-1}(P_D)) \cdot S_{aa}^{\text{noise}} \cdot B}{\eta_{\text{vib}}\,p\,e^{-2\alpha L}\,|H_{\text{clamp}}|^{2}\,|H_{\text{wall}}|^{2} \cdot \sqrt{TB}}.

This is the form in the main document.

G.5 No-detector-beats bound

Theorem G.1. For given (p, d, M_{\text{mat}}, N) and observation budget T, no detector can achieve a lower Q_\ell^{\min} than the deflection-coefficient bound at fixed (P_D, P_{FA}).

Proof. The Neyman–Pearson lemma states that the likelihood-ratio test maximizes P_D at fixed P_{FA}. The deflection coefficient is the maximal SNR achievable by any linear detector. For a Gaussian signal in Gaussian noise, the likelihood-ratio test is linear, and its performance is determined by d^2. Any nonlinear detector can only do worse. CUSUM attains the bound asymptotically by Proposition 4.2. \square

