Appendix H — TDOA CRLB with Random-Signal Model

H.1 Signal model

Let

x_1(t) = s(t) + n_1(t), \qquad x_2(t) = s(t - \Delta t) + n_2(t),

where s(t) is a zero-mean stationary Gaussian process with PSD S_s(f), and n_1, n_2 are independent noise processes with PSDs S_{n_1}(f), S_{n_2}(f).

H.2 Fisher information for time delay

The log-likelihood function for \Delta t given the observations is (Kay 1993, §7.4):

\log L(\Delta t) = -\frac{1}{2}\int \frac{|X_1(f) - X_2(f)e^{-i2\pi f\Delta t}|^{2}}{S_n(f)}df + \text{const},

where X_1, X_2 are the Fourier transforms of the observations and S_n(f) is the effective noise PSD.

The Fisher information is

\mathcal{I}(\Delta t) = -\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{\partial^{2}\log L}{\partial(\Delta t)^{2}}\right].

Computing the second derivative and taking the expectation:

\mathcal{I}(\Delta t) = 2\pi^{2}\int f^{2}\,\frac{S_s^{2}(f)}{S_{n_1}(f)S_{n_2}(f) + S_s(f)(S_{n_1}(f)+S_{n_2}(f))}\,df.

H.3 Coherence weighting

Define the SNR

\text{SNR}(f) = \frac{S_s(f)}{S_n(f)},

and the magnitude-squared coherence

\gamma^{2}(f) = \frac{|S_{x_1x_2}(f)|^{2}}{S_{x_1}(f)S_{x_2}(f)} = \frac{\text{SNR}^{2}(f)}{(1+\text{SNR}(f))^{2}}.

Then the Fisher information can be written as

\mathcal{I}(\Delta t) = 2\pi^{2}T\int f^{2}\gamma^{2}(f)\text{SNR}(f)df.

H.4 Coherence bandwidth

Define the coherence bandwidth

B_{\text{coh}} = \frac{\left(\int \gamma^{2}(f)df\right)^{2}}{\int \gamma^{4}(f)df}.

For a rectangular coherence profile of width B_{\text{coh}} and constant SNR:

\mathcal{I}(\Delta t) = 2\pi^{2}T \cdot \text{SNR} \cdot \frac{B_{\text{coh}}^{3}}{12}.

The CRLB is

\sigma_{\Delta t}^{2} = \mathcal{I}^{-1} = \frac{12}{2\pi^{2}T \cdot \text{SNR} \cdot B_{\text{coh}}^{3}} = \frac{6}{\pi^{2}T \cdot \text{SNR} \cdot B_{\text{coh}}^{3}}.

For the simplified form quoted in the main document:

\sigma_{\Delta t}^{2} \approx \frac{1}{2\pi B_{\text{coh}}\sqrt{\text{SNR} \cdot T \cdot B_{\text{coh}}}}.

Note: The exact constant depends on the shape of the coherence profile. The form above is a convenient approximation for order-of-magnitude estimates.

H.5 Spatial error

The spatial error is

\sigma_{x}^{2} = \frac{c^{2}}{4}\sigma_{\Delta t}^{2} + \frac{\Delta t^{2}}{4}\sigma_{c}^{2}.

The first term is the timing error; the second is the velocity error. On plastic pipes, \sigma_c/c \sim 5–10\%, so the second term dominates.

---

Appendix I — Spatial Error with Velocity Uncertainty

I.1 Derivation

The TDOA estimate is \hat{\Delta t}, and the sound speed estimate is \hat{c}. The position estimate is

\hat{x} = \frac{\hat{c}\hat{\Delta t}}{2}.

By the delta method:

\text{Var}(\hat{x}) = \left(\frac{\partial x}{\partial \Delta t}\right)^{2}\text{Var}(\hat{\Delta t}) + \left(\frac{\partial x}{\partial c}\right)^{2}\text{Var}(\hat{c}) + 2\frac{\partial x}{\partial \Delta t}\frac{\partial x}{\partial c}\text{Cov}(\hat{\Delta t}, \hat{c}).

The partial derivatives are

\frac{\partial x}{\partial \Delta t} = \frac{c}{2}, \qquad \frac{\partial x}{\partial c} = \frac{\Delta t}{2}.

Assuming \hat{\Delta t} and \hat{c} are uncorrelated:

\sigma_{x}^{2} = \frac{c^{2}}{4}\sigma_{\Delta t}^{2} + \frac{\Delta t^{2}}{4}\sigma_{c}^{2}.

I.2 Numerical example

For PEX with c = 300 m/s, \Delta t = 4 ms, \sigma_{\Delta t} = 1 ms, \sigma_c = 30 m/s (10%):

\sigma_{x}^{2} = \frac{300^{2}}{4}(10^{-3})^{2} + \frac{(4\times 10^{-3})^{2}}{4}(30)^{2}
= 0.0225 + 0.0036 = 0.0261\ \text{m}^{2}.

\sigma_x = 0.16\ \text{m}.

The velocity error contributes about 14% of the total variance in this case, but for longer delays or larger velocity uncertainty it can dominate.

---

Appendix J — Energy-Constrained Detection Frontier

J.1 Energy model

Let the node have three power states:

· Acquisition: P_{\text{acq}} (MCU + AFE active)
· Sleep: P_{\text{sleep}} (MCU deep sleep)
· Comparator: P_{\text{cmp}} (always-on analog wake-up)

The duty cycle is D = T_{\text{acq}}/(T_{\text{acq}} + T_{\text{sleep}}). The average power is

\bar{P} = D P_{\text{acq}} + (1-D)P_{\text{sleep}} + P_{\text{cmp}}.

J.2 Effective integration time

The effective integration time is T_{\text{eff}} = \rho T, where \rho = p_{\text{duty}} is the fraction of time the node is actually observing. From Proposition 4.9, the deflection coefficient scales as

d^{2} \propto \rho T \cdot \text{SNR}^{2} \cdot B.

J.3 Energy–delay–P_D frontier

The detection condition is

d \geq Q^{-1}(P_{FA}) - Q^{-1}(P_D) \equiv z.

So

\rho T \cdot \text{SNR}^{2} \cdot B \geq z^{2}.

The minimum energy is E_{\min} = \bar{P} \cdot T. For a given P_D, P_{FA}, \text{SNR}, B:

E_{\min} \propto \frac{z^{2}}{\rho \cdot \text{SNR}^{2} \cdot B}.

Interpretation: The energy required scales inversely with the duty cycle \rho and the squared SNR. This is the fundamental energy–detection trade-off.

J.4 Wake-up comparator as Neyman–Pearson detector

The wake-up comparator is an energy detector with test statistic

E_{\text{band}} = \int_{f_1}^{f_2}|X(f)|^{2}df.

Under H_0, E_{\text{band}}/\sigma_n^{2} follows a chi-square distribution with 2BT_{\text{int}} degrees of freedom. The threshold \lambda is set to achieve a false-wake rate \alpha_w:

P(E_{\text{band}} > \lambda \mid H_0) = \alpha_w.

The miss probability at the target leak energy is

P_{\text{miss}} = P(E_{\text{band}} < \lambda \mid H_1).

The comparator power budget is P_{\text{cmp}} < 50\ \muW.

---

Appendix K — Cross-Node Consistency Test

K.1 TDOA model

For a leak at position x_\ell on a tree graph, the TDOA between nodes i and j is

\tau_{ij}(x_\ell) = \frac{d_i(x_\ell) - d_j(x_\ell)}{c},

where d_i(x_\ell) is the distance along the pipe from node i to the leak.

K.2 Independent TDOAs

For N nodes, there are N-1 independent TDOAs. Select node 1 as reference. The TDOA vector is

\boldsymbol{\tau} = [\tau_{12}, \tau_{13}, \ldots, \tau_{1N}]^{\top}.

The covariance matrix is \Sigma_{\tau}.

K.3 Chi-squared test

The test statistic is

\chi^{2} = (\hat{\boldsymbol{\tau}} - \boldsymbol{\tau}(x_\ell))^{\top}\Sigma_{\tau}^{-1}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\tau}} - \boldsymbol{\tau}(x_\ell)).

Under H_0 (consistent hypothesis), \chi^{2} follows a chi-square distribution with N-2 degrees of freedom (because one degree of freedom is lost to the unknown leak position x_\ell).

The consistency test rejects at level \alpha if

\chi^{2} > \chi^{2}_{1-\alpha}(N-2).

K.4 Multi-node detection logic

Multi-node detection requires:

1. A local CUSUM alarm at one or more nodes.
2. A passing consistency test on the TDOA vector.

The combined false-alarm rate is controlled by the product of the local false-alarm rates and the consistency-test level.

---

Appendix L — Statistical Power

L.1 Wilson confidence interval

For binomial detection with true probability P_D and N trials, the Wilson score interval half-width is

\Delta_{95} = \frac{z_{0.975}}{\sqrt{N}}\sqrt{\frac{P_D(1-P_D)}{N} + \frac{z_{0.975}^{2}}{4N^{2}}} \cdot \frac{1}{1 + z_{0.975}^{2}/N}.

For large N, this simplifies to

\Delta_{95} \approx 1.96\sqrt{\frac{P_D(1-P_D)}{N}}.

L.2 Sample size requirements

At P_D = 0.9:

· N = 20: \Delta_{95} \approx 13\%
· N = 100: \Delta_{95} \approx 5.9\%
· N = 400: \Delta_{95} \approx 2.9\%

Consequence: Any comparison of two configurations requires at least 100 trials per condition to achieve a 95% confidence interval half-width below 6%.

L.3 Mixed-effects model

For a hierarchical design with material as a random effect, the model is

y_{ijk} = \mu + \alpha_i + \beta_j + \gamma_{ij} + u_k + \epsilon_{ijk},

where \alpha_i is the pressure effect, \beta_j is the material effect, \gamma_{ij} is the interaction, u_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_u^{2}) is the random material effect, and \epsilon_{ijk} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^{2}) is the residual.

---

Appendix M — Clock Synchronization

M.1 Clock drift model

Let the local clock at node i have frequency offset \delta_i and drift rate \dot{\delta}_i. The clock error at time t is

\epsilon_i(t) = \epsilon_i(0) + \delta_i t + \frac{1}{2}\dot{\delta}_i t^{2}.

For a crystal oscillator with \pm 20–50 ppm stability:

|\delta_i| \leq 50 \times 10^{-6}.

Over T = 1 s:

|\epsilon_i(T)| \leq 50\ \mu\text{s}.

M.2 Impact on TDOA

The TDOA error due to clock drift is

\sigma_{\Delta t}^{\text{clock}} = \sqrt{\sigma_{\epsilon_1}^{2} + \sigma_{\epsilon_2}^{2}}.

For copper (c = 3900 m/s), the spatial error is

\sigma_x^{\text{clock}} = \frac{c}{2}\sigma_{\Delta t}^{\text{clock}} = 1950 \times 70 \times 10^{-6} = 0.14\ \text{m}.

For PEX (c = 300 m/s):

\sigma_x^{\text{clock}} = 150 \times 70 \times 10^{-6} = 0.01\ \text{m}.

So clock drift is acceptable on both materials at T = 1 s.

M.3 Online drift estimation

The clock drift can be estimated from cross-correlated ambient noise. The procedure:

1. Compute the cross-correlation between two nodes over a long window.
2. Track the peak position over time.
3. Fit a linear model to the peak position vs. time.
4. Use the slope as the relative clock drift estimate.

This doubles as the sound-speed calibration.

---

Appendix N — Tree Topology Identifiability

N.1 Tree graph model

Let the plumbing network be a tree graph \mathcal{T} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}) with nodes v \in \mathcal{V} and edges e \in \mathcal{E}. Each edge has length L_e and sound speed c_e.

N.2 TDOA on a tree

For a leak at position x_\ell on edge e_\ell, the distance from node i to the leak is

d_i(x_\ell) = \text{dist}_{\mathcal{T}}(i, x_\ell),

the shortest path length along the tree.

N.3 Identifiability condition

Proposition N.1. The leak position x_\ell is identifiable from the TDOA vector \boldsymbol{\tau} = [\tau_{12}, \ldots, \tau_{1N}] if and only if the map x_\ell \mapsto \boldsymbol{\tau}(x_\ell) is injective on the tree.

Proof. Identifiability is equivalent to injectivity of the forward map. If two distinct positions x_\ell \neq x_\ell' produce the same TDOA vector, then the positions are indistinguishable. \square

N.4 Branch point ambiguity

At a branch point v_b with degree d_b \geq 3, the TDOA vector may be ambiguous if the leak is exactly at the branch. The identifiability condition requires that the branch point be resolved by amplitude decay ratios or by additional sensors.

N.5 Amplitude decay

The amplitude at node i decays as

A_i = A_0 e^{-\alpha d_i(x_\ell)}.

The ratio of amplitudes at two nodes is

\frac{A_i}{A_j} = e^{-\alpha(d_i - d_j)}.

This provides an independent constraint on the leak position, resolving branch-point ambiguities.

